# Rust production deployment log

All timestamps UTC. User authorized an in-place Rust deployment attempt on 2026-09-13. No CloudFormation update, instance replacement, volume deletion, instance upgrade or continuous Bedrock configuration is authorized.

## Temporary login behavior

The first login cache file was written 13:12:09Z with access-token expiry 13:27:09Z. Profile `cds-login` correctly points to the existing login session; its SHA-256 matches the cache filename; owner/mode are correct; DPoP key and refresh token are present; no `AWS_LOGIN_CACHE_DIRECTORY` or `~/.aws/credentials` override exists; UTC clock was correct. A command at 13:25 received `CreateOAuth2Token INVALID_REQUEST` during proactive refresh. Pending alternate login commands were stopped before authorization, without logout.

The user's refreshed cache was written 13:29:15Z with access expiry 13:44:15Z. Serialized calls succeeded until the app's 119th Titan vector, then refresh failed at 13:39:18Z—inside the CLI's five-minute proactive-refresh window—with the same invalid-grant response. No concurrent AWS process was running, and the cache mtime did not advance. A refresh race is therefore unlikely. AWS documents 15-minute access credentials that should refresh for up to 12 hours ([CLI guide](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/cli-configure-sign-in.html)); this behavior is not expected expiry.

A later static audit found a real regional failure mode in open AWS CLI issue [#10613](https://github.com/aws/aws-cli/issues/10613). AWS maintainer Ryan FitzSimmons states: “SignIn enforces that the refresh token is redeemed in the same region it was generated. When you make a request and the token is expired, the AWS CLI (and other SDKs) will attempt to refresh the token in the region of the request.” AWS CLI source confirms that `aws login --region R` creates the Sign-In client in `R`, but saves the profile region only when the CLI prompted for it; an explicit `--region` is not persisted. Botocore refresh creates its Sign-In client without a separate issuer-region override, so it uses the active request/session region.

Local static facts: `cds-login` has no configured region; no `AWS_REGION` or `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION` override is set; the cache JSON has no separate issuer-region field. The refresh token is opaque. The ID token is a JWT, and a bounded in-process extraction printed only its nonsecret `iss` claim: `https://us-east-2.signin.aws.amazon.com/signin`. No token or other payload claim was logged. The successful login issuer is therefore `us-east-2`.

The Titan subprocess explicitly used `--region us-west-2`, so its refresh attempted west against an east-2 token. The parent's later unpaid STS retry used `--region us-east-1` and failed with the same error, also against the east-2 token. Both negative results are region mismatches. Initial access working cross-region followed by refresh failing at the proactive boundary matches the independently documented behavior in AWS CLI #10613. This is strong causal evidence for region mismatch, though a fresh matched-region boundary test remains the final discriminator. Closed issue [#10267](https://github.com/aws/aws-cli/issues/10267) was a different service incident.

The next bounded human test must use `us-west-2` for both a fresh login and the unpaid STS request, matching the Titan workload. Confirm the authorization host is `us-west-2.signin.aws.amazon.com`, then make one serial STS call in `us-west-2` after the refresh boundary. The existing token could instead be refreshed in its `us-east-2` issuer region and exported as short-lived credentials for a west workload, but this only routes around the mismatch and does not support a long collection loop. Do not combine issuer and request regions, poll authentication, run debug logging or print token values.

